\ A parameter whose call sites disagree stays opaque, so its arithmetic keeps the
\ polymorphic path. This is the negative control for
\ `param_type_inference_fib_strict.1z`.
\
\ `twice` is called once with a fixnum and once with a float, so agree-only
\ narrowing proves nothing about `x`. `dup +` then compiles with both operands
\ opaque, and under the lock its cold arm resolves through the frozen dispatch
\ table. Had the conflict check been missing, `x` would have narrowed to whichever
\ call site was seen last and the other call would tag-check the wrong tag.
\
\ `param_type_inference_narrowing.1z` covers the same word without the lock, where
\ the pass cannot type a fixnum arithmetic result at all. Under the lock it can, so
\ this is the one configuration where the check is load-bearing.

kind: generic ( x -- s ) [ drop "other" ] ;
kind: method{ fixnum } [ drop "fixnum" ] ;
kind: method{ float } [ drop "float" ] ;

twice: ( x -- y ) [ dup + ] ;

5 twice kind print-line
2.5 twice kind print-line
5 twice 10 = [ "fixnum double ok" ] [ "fixnum double wrong" ] if print-line
2.5 twice 5.0 = [ "float double ok" ] [ "float double wrong" ] if print-line
